---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
    { title: "Best Electric Pressure Washers", url: "/best-electric-pressure-washer/" },
    { title: "Electric vs Gas Pressure Washers", url: "/electric-vs-gas-pressure-washer/" },
    { title: "What PSI Do I Need?", url: "/what-psi-pressure-washer-do-i-need/" },
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-06", modified: "2026-09-06" });
const title = "Cordless vs Corded Pressure Washer: Which Wins?";
const description =
  "Battery pressure washers are genuinely capable now, but runtime is still the limit. Corded, cordless, hybrid and wall-mounted compared honestly.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Cordless vs Corded",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/cordless-vs-corded-pressure-washer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are cordless pressure washers any good?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The best of them genuinely out-perform mid-range corded units, and the siphon hose that draws from a bucket or a lake opens up jobs corded machines cannot do. The limit is runtime: a pressure washer is a continuous full-load draw, so plan on managing power modes and swapping packs.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does a battery pressure washer run?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Less than the quoted figure, which is usually measured at the lowest power setting with the largest battery. At full pressure on a mid-size pack, expect enough for a car, some furniture or a section of fence. A full driveway is a stretch even on high-capacity batteries.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a cordless pressure washer clean a driveway?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not realistically in one session. Flat concrete is the most demanding job in the category because water volume does all the debris clearing, and that is exactly what drains a battery fastest. If driveways are on your list, buy corded and treat cordless as a second machine.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between a power cleaner and a pressure washer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Pressure. Power cleaners like the Worx Hydroshot produce roughly 300 to 600 PSI, against 1,500 PSI and up for a real pressure washer. Flow can be similar, so they rinse bikes and boots well, but they will not clean a deck or a driveway at any dwell time.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a cordless pressure washer draw water from a bucket?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most include a siphon hose and filter for exactly that, and it works from a bucket, barrel, water butt, lake or stream. A five-gallon bucket is a meaningful amount of cleaning at these flow rates. Keep the intake off the bottom so it does not draw sediment into the pump.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a hybrid pressure washer worth the extra cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Mainly if you already own batteries in that system. Running from mains for long sessions and from a pack for quick ones removes the runtime objection completely, but it is the most expensive way to buy an electric machine, and buying into a new platform at full price rarely justifies it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are wall-mounted pressure washers worth it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "If you do frequent short jobs at one location, yes, because nothing to drag out means it actually gets used. The trade is installation near both water and power, and a machine that never leaves the wall. For a single property with a lot of small cleaning tasks it is an underrated format.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does cold weather affect a battery pressure washer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, noticeably. Lithium packs lose usable capacity in the cold, which bites hardest on the winter jobs where a bucket-fed cordless machine is most useful, like rinsing road salt off a car. Keeping spare packs indoors until you need them recovers most of the difference.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "cordless vs corded pressure washer",
      "battery pressure washer",
      "hybrid pressure washer",
      "tools & equipment",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Electric Pressure Washers",
    link: "/best-electric-pressure-washer/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Comparison
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Field Tested
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Cordless vs Corded <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Pressure Washers</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Electric used to mean one thing. It now covers four quite different machines, and picking the wrong format is a more expensive mistake than picking the wrong pressure rating.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#four-formats"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            Compare the Formats
          </a>
          <a
            href="#faq"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the FAQ
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="10.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#four-formats" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Four Formats</a>
          <a href="#runtime" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Runtime Reality</a>
          <a href="#water-source" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Siphon Advantage</a>
          <a href="#power-cleaners" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Power Cleaners</a>
          <a href="#battery-platform" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Battery Platform</a>
          <a href="#which-format" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Choosing a Format</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Battery machines have stopped being novelties and started being tools. The best of them out-perform mid-range corded units, and they still cannot do a driveway. Which of those facts matters depends entirely on what you clean.
        </p>

        <div id="four-formats" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Four Formats
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before comparing, it is worth being clear that there are four distinct machines competing for the word electric, not two.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Corded</h3>

          <p>
            The default, and still the right answer for most people. Unlimited runtime, the lowest cost per cleaning unit, and nothing to charge. The trade is that you are tethered to both an outlet and a pressurised water source, which around a typical house means dragging an extension cord and a garden hose to wherever you are working.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cordless</h3>

          <p>
            Two things make battery machines worth the premium: no outlet to find, and a siphon hose that draws from a bucket, lake or water butt when there is no spigot. The limit is runtime. Expect to manage power modes and swap batteries, and expect a full driveway to be a stretch even on high-capacity packs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hybrid</h3>

          <p>
            A newer format that runs from either mains power or batteries, giving cordless freedom for quick jobs and unlimited runtime for long ones. It is the most flexible option and the most expensive, and it only makes sense if you already own batteries in that manufacturer's system or intend to.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wall-Mounted</h3>

          <p>
            A permanently installed unit with a retractable hose reel, mounted in a garage or on an exterior wall. Nothing to drag out and nothing to store, which in practice means it gets used for five-minute jobs a cart machine never gets used for. It requires installation near water and power, and it is not going anywhere with you.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Format</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Runtime</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Cost</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Corded</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unlimited</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most households</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$150-430</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cordless</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Battery-limited</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No outlet, no spigot</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$300-800</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hybrid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Either</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Existing battery owners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$550-800</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wall-mounted</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unlimited</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Frequent short jobs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$400-700 plus fitting</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Runtime: The Honest Numbers
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is where cordless machines are most often oversold and where buyers are most often disappointed. A pressure washer is a continuous full-load draw, which is the hardest possible duty for a battery. It is nothing like a drill that works in bursts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manufacturers quote runtime at the lowest power setting with a large battery, which is a real figure describing a mode you will rarely use. At full pressure with a mid-size pack, plan on considerably less.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Job</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Realistic Cordless Feasibility</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rinsing muddy boots, bikes, a dog</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Easy, and the format is ideal</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Washing one car</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Comfortable on a decent battery</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Patio furniture and a grill</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Comfortable</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">A section of fence</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Feasible with a spare pack</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">A small deck</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Feasible, expect at least one swap</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">A full driveway</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A stretch even on high-capacity packs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">A whole house of siding</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not realistically, plan on corded</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule of Thumb</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Cordless suits jobs you finish before you would have gone to fetch the cord.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">That is the actual value proposition, and it is a real one. The five-minute tasks that never justify unrolling an extension cord and a hose are exactly the tasks a battery machine does brilliantly. Judge it by how many of those you have rather than by whether it can survive a driveway, because it cannot.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="water-source" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Siphon Advantage
          </h2>

          <p>
            The freedom people notice first is the missing cord. The freedom that turns out to matter more often is the missing hose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most cordless machines include a siphon hose with a filter that draws from any open water: a bucket, a barrel, a water butt, a lake, a stream. That removes the second tether, and it opens up jobs a corded machine simply cannot do without a lot of hose.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Rinsing a boat, kayak or paddleboard at a launch site where there is no tap at all.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Cleaning muddy bikes or boots at a trailhead or the far end of a garden.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Washing a car at a house where the outside tap is on the wrong side of the building.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Working on an allotment, a stable, or any outbuilding with water in a butt but no pressurised supply.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Cleaning gutters and outdoor furniture at a holiday cottage or caravan with limited services.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Rinsing salt off equipment on a driveway in winter, from a bucket of warm water.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            A five-gallon bucket is a meaningful amount of cleaning at the flow rates these machines run. It is not enough for a driveway, but it is plenty for a bike, a set of chairs or a muddy dog, and a bucket can be refilled anywhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caution: the siphon filter matters. Drawing from a pond or a water butt means drawing grit, and grit reaching the pump does the same damage there as anywhere else. Keep the filter clean and keep the intake off the bottom of whatever you are drawing from.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-cleaners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Power Cleaners Are Not Pressure Washers
          </h2>

          <p>
            A separate class of tool has appeared that looks like a cordless pressure washer and is not one. This is the single most common mis-purchase in the category, and the listings do very little to prevent it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Power cleaners are self-contained wands that typically produce 300 to 600 PSI, against 1,500 PSI and up for a real pressure washer. Flow can be comparable, which is why they rinse a bike, a kayak or muddy boots perfectly well, and why they are useless on a driveway.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Power Cleaner</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cordless Pressure Washer</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical pressure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">300-600 PSI</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500-2,300 PSI</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rinsing bikes and boots</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Washing a car</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Adequate rinse, weak on film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Genuinely capable</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Decks and fences</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slowly</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Draws from a bucket</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Size and weight</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wand only, very light</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A machine plus a wand</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">How to Spot One</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If the pressure is quoted in the hundreds, it is a power cleaner.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Whatever the product title says, a figure like 320 or 580 PSI describes a rinsing tool, not a pressure washer. That is not a criticism, because they are genuinely good at what they do and pleasantly portable. It is only a problem when someone buys one expecting to clean a patio with it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="battery-platform" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Battery Platform Question
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buying a cordless pressure washer is rarely just buying a pressure washer. It is a decision about which battery system you are committing to, and that decision outlives the machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own several batteries in a manufacturer's system, a bare-tool cordless washer is dramatically cheaper than the kit price and the runtime problem is already half solved, because you own spares. If you do not, you are buying into a platform at full price, and the batteries are most of what you are paying for.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Prefer a bare tool if you already have packs. The saving is often close to half the kit price.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Check the minimum pack size the machine needs. Small packs may run it briefly or not at all under full pressure.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Buy high-capacity packs specifically for this tool. A pressure washer is the most demanding thing in most battery lineups.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Expect batteries to run warm and to want a rest before recharging, which lengthens the real cycle between sessions.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Account for cold weather, which reduces usable capacity noticeably on the winter jobs where a bucket-fed machine is most useful.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Check whether the charger you own is the fast one. Runtime is only half the story if a recharge takes several hours.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Hybrids are the honest answer to the platform question for people who already own batteries. Running from mains for a long session and from a pack for a quick one removes the runtime objection entirely, and it is the reason the format exists. It is also the most expensive way to buy an electric pressure washer, which is why it only makes sense inside a system you already own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-format" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Choosing Your Format
          </h2>

          <p>
            Work down this list and stop at the first row that describes you. The formats sort more cleanly than most buying decisions.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">If This Is You</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Buy</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">You clean a driveway, patio or whole house</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Corded</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">You mostly rinse bikes, boots, kayaks and dogs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cordless, or a power cleaner</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">There is no outdoor tap where you need to clean</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cordless with a siphon hose</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">You already own batteries and want both</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hybrid</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">You do lots of five-minute jobs at one location</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wall-mounted</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">You want one machine to do everything</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Corded, and accept the cord</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Budget is the deciding factor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Corded, every time</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Cost per cleaning unit is where corded wins decisively and quietly. The same money buys substantially more capability with a cord attached, and for a machine that lives in a garage next to an outlet, the cord is a minor inconvenience rather than a real constraint. That is why it remains the default recommendation in our <a href="/best-electric-pressure-washer/">electric pressure washer guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do go corded, the cord itself deserves thought. An undersized extension cord causes a voltage drop that makes the motor draw more current and run hotter, which shortens its life on exactly the machines least able to afford it. Our <a href="/pressure-washer-extension-cord-size/">cord sizing guide</a> covers the gauge and length limits, and they are stricter than most people assume.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Power Cleaner Expecting a Pressure Washer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Power cleaners produce 300 to 600 PSI against 1,500 and up for a real machine. They rinse bikes, boots and kayaks beautifully and will not touch a patio. If the listing quotes pressure in the hundreds, you are looking at a rinsing tool whatever the product title claims.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Quoted Runtime</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Manufacturers quote runtime at the lowest power setting with the largest battery. At full pressure with a mid-size pack you will get a fraction of it, because a pressure washer is a continuous full-load draw and the hardest duty in any battery lineup. Budget for at least one spare pack.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Cordless for Driveway Work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A full driveway is a stretch even on high-capacity packs, and a whole house of siding is not realistic at all. Cordless suits jobs you finish in the time it would have taken to fetch and unroll the cord. Judge it on how many of those you have, not on whether it could theoretically manage more.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Which Battery Platform You Are Joining</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The packs are most of the price, so buying into a new system at full kit price is an expensive way to get a pressure washer. If you already own batteries, look for a bare tool in that system, which often costs close to half the kit and solves the runtime problem because you already have spares.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the Siphon Filter Draw Grit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Drawing from a water butt, a pond or a bucket that has been standing means drawing sediment, and grit reaching the pump erodes it exactly as it would on a corded machine. Keep the intake off the bottom, keep the filter clean, and rinse it after drawing from anything untreated.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cordless pressure washers any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best of them genuinely out-perform mid-range corded units, and the siphon hose that draws from a bucket or a lake opens up jobs corded machines cannot do. The limit is runtime: a pressure washer is a continuous full-load draw, so plan on managing power modes and swapping packs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a battery pressure washer run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Less than the quoted figure, which is usually measured at the lowest power setting with the largest battery. At full pressure on a mid-size pack, expect enough for a car, some furniture or a section of fence. A full driveway is a stretch even on high-capacity batteries.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a cordless pressure washer clean a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not realistically in one session. Flat concrete is the most demanding job in the category because water volume does all the debris clearing, and that is exactly what drains a battery fastest. If driveways are on your list, buy corded and treat cordless as a second machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a power cleaner and a pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pressure. Power cleaners like the Worx Hydroshot produce roughly 300 to 600 PSI, against 1,500 PSI and up for a real pressure washer. Flow can be similar, so they rinse bikes and boots well, but they will not clean a deck or a driveway at any dwell time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a cordless pressure washer draw water from a bucket?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most include a siphon hose and filter for exactly that, and it works from a bucket, barrel, water butt, lake or stream. A five-gallon bucket is a meaningful amount of cleaning at these flow rates. Keep the intake off the bottom so it does not draw sediment into the pump.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a hybrid pressure washer worth the extra cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mainly if you already own batteries in that system. Running from mains for long sessions and from a pack for quick ones removes the runtime objection completely, but it is the most expensive way to buy an electric machine, and buying into a new platform at full price rarely justifies it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are wall-mounted pressure washers worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you do frequent short jobs at one location, yes, because nothing to drag out means it actually gets used. The trade is installation near both water and power, and a machine that never leaves the wall. For a single property with a lot of small cleaning tasks it is an underrated format.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does cold weather affect a battery pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, noticeably. Lithium packs lose usable capacity in the cold, which bites hardest on the winter jobs where a bucket-fed cordless machine is most useful, like rinsing road salt off a car. Keeping spare packs indoors until you need them recovers most of the difference.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

