          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Corded remains the right answer for most households. It gives unlimited runtime and the lowest cost per cleaning unit, and for a machine that lives near an outlet the cord is an inconvenience rather than a constraint. Anything involving a driveway, a patio or a house full of siding is corded work.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Cordless earns its place on the jobs you would finish before you had finished unrolling the cord: bikes, boots, dogs, one car, patio furniture. Its underrated advantage is the siphon hose, which draws from a bucket, barrel or lake and removes the water tether as well as the power one. Runtime is the real limit, and the quoted figures assume the lowest power setting with the biggest pack.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Before buying either, check what you are actually looking at. A tool quoting pressure in the hundreds is a power cleaner, which rinses beautifully and cleans nothing stubborn. And if you already own batteries in a system, look for a bare tool or a hybrid in that system, because the packs are most of what a cordless kit costs.
          </p>
        </div>


